#X obj 230 93 cnv 1 535 525 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -237178 -1 0;
#X text 240 98 Note: the default PWM frequency is 50 Hz.;
#X text 240 123 *Servo control*;
#X text 240 148 Many digital servos run on a 20 ms pulse-width duty
cycle (50 Hz update frequency) and do not use the entire pulse width
for their control.;
#X text 240 203 Typically \, 2.5% of the 20 ms pulse width (0.5 ms)
represents a −90° offset \, and 12.5% of the 20 ms pulse width (2.5
ms) represents a +90° offset.;
#X text 240 258 Be aware that driving some 180° servos too far past
their −90°/+90° operational range can cause a small plastic limiter
pin to break off and get stuck inside the servo's gearing \, which
could potentially cause the servo to become jammed and no longer function.;
#X obj 230 263 cnv 1 2 75 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -221520 -1 0;
#X text 240 343 Continuous servos operate in much the same way as 180°
servos \, but instead of the 2.5%/12.5% pulse width controlling a −90°/+90°
offset \, it controls a −1x/+1x speed multiplier \, where 0x means
parked/no movement and −1x/+1x mean maximum speed in either direction.;
#X text 240 428 To connect to the device \, set the @connect property
to 1\.;
#X obj 230 433 cnv 1 2 15 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -158925 -1 0;
#X text 240 453 Typical use cases:;
#X text 250 478 • Multi-servo robotics — driving up to 16 servo motors
from a single module \, for example in a walking robot \, an animatronic
figure or a robotic arm with several degrees of freedom.;
#X text 250 548 • LED and lighting control — using the PCA9685 as a
16-channel PWM driver for LEDs \, dimmers or small lamps \, with independent
duty cycle per channel.;
#X text 50 666 180° servo example;
